. 
.         save "$DATA/h1_dataset.dta", replace // save merged dataset
file C:\Users\dalbrecht\Research
    Projects\ManyDaughters\ManyDaughters_DataPreprocessing/data/derived_confi
    > dential/5%-sample/h1_dataset.dta saved

. 
. 
. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. /* REMARKS: 
> 
> 1. The provided data set pl does not include the variable pla0009_h although 
> it was listed on paneldata.org as being part of the data set. We use variable
> s pla0009_v2 and pla0009_v3 to construct pla0009_h ourselves. 
> 
> 2. Our pre-analysis plan did not specify how we deal with cases where individ
> uals provided inconsistent responses for their year of birth or sex. In this 
> code, we set the corresponding variable to missing if an individual provided 
> inconsistent responses across the years. 
> 
> */
. 
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES Part I* (Regressors)
. 
.         * Generate dummy variable for whether participant has a first-born da
> ughter (1) or first-born son (0)
.         gen d_daughter = kidsex01==2

.         replace d_daughter=. if kidsex01<1 | kidsex01==. // set invalid respo
> nses to missing
(10,637 real changes made, 10,637 to missing)

.         
.         * Generate variable for individual being male based on plh0009 (consi
> dering sex observations across years)
.         gen pla0009_h = pla0009_v2
(8,122 missing values generated)

.         replace pla0009_v3 = -2 if  pla0009_v3==3
(0 real changes made)

.         replace pla0009_h = pla0009_v3 if syear>=2019
(3,051 real changes made)

.         replace pla0009_h=. if pla0009_h<0 // set invalid responses to missin
> g
(2,483 real changes made, 2,483 to missing)

.         bysort pid: egen sex_pla = mean(pla0009_h)
(476 missing values generated)

.         gen male = sex_pla==1

.         replace male = . if sex_pla!=1&sex_pla!=2 // replace with missings if
>  inconsistent responses across years 
(513 real changes made, 513 to missing)

.         
.         * Generate categorical variable for age in 2012 (considering year of 
> birth observations across years)
.         replace ple0010_h=. if ple0010_h<0 // set invalid responses to missin
> g
(1,493 real changes made, 1,493 to missing)

.         bysort pid: egen yearofbirth_min = min(ple0010_h)
(781 missing values generated)

.         bysort pid: egen yearofbirth_max = max(ple0010_h)
(781 missing values generated)

.         gen yearofbirth = yearofbirth_max if yearofbirth_min==yearofbirth_max
>  // results in missing if inconsistent responses across years
(971 missing values generated)

.         gen age_2012 = 2012-yearofbirth
(971 missing values generated)

.         gen age_cat = 1 if age_2012>=18 & age_2012<=24
(32,523 missing values generated)

.         replace age_cat = 2 if age_2012>=25 & age_2012<=30
(3,062 real changes made)

.         replace age_cat = 3 if age_2012>=31 & age_2012<=40
(5,186 real changes made)

.         replace age_cat = 4 if age_2012>=41 & age_2012<=50
(6,406 real changes made)

.         replace age_cat = 5 if age_2012>=51 & age_2012<=64
(7,181 real changes made)

.         replace age_cat = 6 if age_2012>=65 & age_2012!=.
(6,827 real changes made)

. 
. 
. * FILTERS *
.  
.         * Exclude participants who have a missing/invalid value for one of th
> e variables that are used to construct the outcome variable below
.         keep if plh0301>0 & plh0301!=. & plh0303>0 & plh0303!=. & plh0307>0 &
>  plh0307!=.
(34,864 observations deleted)

.         
.         * Exclude participants with missing values in regressors (which also 
> excludes individuals below 18 based on age variable and individuals without c
> hildren)
.         keep if d_daughter!=. & male!=. & age_cat!=.
(95 observations deleted)

.         
.         * Exclude participants without hid
.         keep if hid!=. 
(0 observations deleted)

.         
.         * Exclude participants whose first-born child was born in 2012 or lat
> er
.         keep if kidgeb01<2012 & kidgeb01>0 
(2 observations deleted)

.         
.         
. * GENERATE VARIABLES Part II* (Outcome variable; generate here since standard
> ization uses filtered sample)
.         
.         * Generate outcome variable based on plh0301, plh0303, and plh0307
.         gen plh0303_reverse = 5-plh0303 // reverse code variable such that hi
> gher values mean more gender equality

.         foreach var in plh0301 plh0303_reverse plh0307 { // standardize input
>  variables
  2.                 gen base = `var' if d_daughter==0 
  3.                 egen mbase = mean(base)
  4.                 egen sdbase = sd(base)
  5.                 replace `var' = (`var'-mbase)/sdbase
  6.                 drop base mbase sdbase
  7.         }
(123 missing values generated)
variable plh0301 was byte now float
(267 real changes made)
(123 missing values generated)
(267 real changes made)
(123 missing values generated)
variable plh0307 was byte now float
(267 real changes made)

.         gen attitude = (plh0301 + plh0303_reverse + plh0307)/3 // create outc
> ome variable

.         
.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 *
. 
.         * Description of model: We conduct an OLS regression, where we regres
> s the outcome variable (gender equality attitudes) on a dummy for whether the
>  first-born child is female. We additionally include a dummy for whether the 
> respondent is male and a categorical variable for the age of the respondent i
> n 2012 as regressors. We cluster standard errors on the household level. To t
> est the hypothesis, we test whether we can reject that the coefficient of the
>  dummy for the first-born child being female is smaller than or equal to zero
>  at the 5% significance level (based on a one-sided t-test).
. 
.         reg attitude d_daughter male i.age_cat, vce(cluster hid)        

Linear regression                               Number of obs     =        267
                                                F(6, 172)         =          .
                                                Prob > F          =          .
                                                R-squared         =     0.0743
                                                Root MSE          =     .77241

                                  (Std. err. adjusted for 173 clusters in hid)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
             |               Robust
    attitude | Coefficient  std. err.      t    P>|t|     [95% conf. interval]
-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------
  d_daughter |   .0969185   .1096968     0.88   0.378    -.1196068    .3134437
        male |  -.2829052   .0885958    -3.19   0.002    -.4577803   -.1080301
             |
     age_cat |
          2  |   .1317469   .3610956     0.36   0.716    -.5810023    .8444962
          3  |   .6212353   .3958483     1.57   0.118    -.1601108    1.402581
          4  |   .6313934   .3983061     1.59   0.115    -.1548039    1.417591
          5  |   .5956576   .4413251     1.35   0.179    -.2754529    1.466768
          6  |   .7889895   .3966186     1.99   0.048     .0061229    1.571856
             |
       _cons |  -.4513431   .3898064    -1.16   0.249    -1.220763    .3180771
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.         
. * EXTRACT AND EXPORT RESULTS *
. 
.         * Extract the required statistics
. 
.                 * (i) coefficient estimate
.                 local coefficient = _b[d_daughter]

. 
.                 * (ii) standard error
.                 local se = _se[d_daughter]

. 
.                 * (iii) family of the test statistic (e.g. z, t, F, Chi²)
.                 local test_stat_family = "t"

. 
.                 * (iv) residual and model degrees of freedom (DFs) if applica
> ble
.                 local df_residual = e(df_r)

.                 local df_model = e(df_m)

. 
.                 * (v) p-value
.                 local p_value = _b[d_daughter] / _se[d_daughter]

.                 local p_value = ttail(e(df_r), `p_value')

. 
.                 * (vi) number of observations
.                 local num_obs = e(N)

. 
.                 * (vii) number of participants
.                 quietly levelsof pid if e(sample), local(participants)

.                 local num_participants : word count `participants'

. 
.                 * (viii) interpretation of directionality
.                 local interpretation "posititve coefficient is in line with h
> ypothesis"

.                 
.                 
.                 * (iv) check that sample size does not fall below pre-specifi
> ed minimum 
.                 if `num_participants' < 1500 {
.                         local remark "DO NOT INTERPRET RESULTS SINCE EFFECTIV
> E SAMPLE SIZE IS BELOW PRE-SPECIFIED MINIMUM (1500)"
.                 } 

.                 else {
.                         local remark ""
.                 }

. 
.         // Display the saved statistics
.         di "Coefficient: `coefficient'"
Coefficient: .0969184576577003

.         di "Standard Error: `se'"
Standard Error: .1096968017474923

.         di "Test Statistic Family: `test_stat_family'"
Test Statistic Family: t

.         di "Residual Degrees of Freedom: `df_residual'"
